text:
 Upload and download files

 Configure the backend

Set the S3 endpoint, region, physical bucket and operator credentials. These credentials remain server-side. Start local MinIO and initialize its physical bucket through the Compose setup service.

Create logical buckets in Studio with a name, public/private visibility, size limit and optional MIME allowlist.

 Upload lifecycle


const result = await qb.storage.upload(bucketId, 'notes/report.txt', file);
console.log(result.data.id);


The SDK creates an upload intent, then sends the bytes to the authenticated gateway. The object is visible only after S3 persistence and expected-size verification succeed. Failed objects remain unavailable and can be retried.

The initial gateway caps uploads at 100 MiB and buffers each bounded upload. Streaming and resumable uploads are later enhancements. Choose smaller bucket limits for constrained hosts.

 Ownership policies

Only authenticated users can upload. Private objects are read and modified by their owners. Public buckets allow reads of ready objects; writes remain owner-restricted. The metadata owner check and current session are enforced on each request.

Object bytes stay in S3 while searchable metadata stays in the branch database. Keys are normalized, and project/branch object prefixes are independent.

 Download and delete


const blob = await qb.storage.download(objectId);
const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
// Use the URL in your UI, then release it:
URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
await qb.storage.remove(objectId);


Delete marks the object as deleting before removing its backend bytes. Metadata is finalized only after cleanup succeeds. Private responses use no-store caching.

 Recovery boundaries

A database backup includes metadata, not object bytes. Enable object-backend backups separately. Restored branch metadata cannot assume a new branch prefix contains the source bytes; perform an explicit object restore before expecting restored files to be available.

 React quickstart

 Before you begin

Create the task table, ownership policies and publishable key from [your first project](/docs/getting-started/first-project). Use React 19. The runnable example is in examples/react.


bun run --cwd examples/react dev


For an external application, run bun run sdk:pack in QB Flex, then install both artifacts and their React peers from your Vite React application:


npm install /path/to/QBFlex/dist/packages/qb-flex-client-0.1.0.tgz /path/to/QBFlex/dist/packages/qb-flex-react-0.1.0.tgz react@19.3.0 react-dom@19.3.0


Replace the paths with the actual absolute paths. npm installs the adapter's pinned TanStack Query dependency. Bun 1.4.2 attempts to resolve the unpublished client peer from the registry when installing these local tarballs, so use npm for this external artifact workflow. Configure the application's exact origin in Studio.

 Create the client once


import { createClient } from '@qb-flex/client';
import { QBProvider } from '@qb-flex/react';
import type { Database } from './qb-flex.types';

const client = createClientDatabase({
  url: 'http://localhost:7612',
  projectId: 'YOUR_PROJECT_ID',
  branch: 'primary',
  publishableKey: 'YOUR_PUBLISHABLE_KEY',
});

export function App() {
  return QBProvider client={client}Tasks //QBProvider;
}


The provider supplies a shared client and its own TanStack Query cache. You may pass an existing queryClient when integrating into an application that already uses Query.

 Read data with a custom hook


import { useQBQuery } from '@qb-flex/react';

function Tasks() {
  const query = useQBQuery({
    queryKey: ['tasks'],
    queryFn: (_, signal) = client.from('tasks')
      .select(['id', 'title'])
      .abortSignal(signal)
      .execute(),
  });
  if (query.isPending) return pLoading tasks…/p;
  if (query.error) return p role="alert"{query.error.message}/p;
  return ul{query.data.data.map(task =
    li key={task.id}{task.title}/li
  )}/ul;
}


Use generated database declarations to retain column and row inference. Include filters and sort choices in queryKey whenever they affect the result.

 Next steps

Add [authentication](/docs/sdk/react/authentication), [mutations and realtime](/docs/sdk/react/data), then [file handling](/docs/sdk/react/storage). Every hook requires the provider; a missing provider produces a clear configuration error.

 React authentication

 Read the shared session


import { useQBAuth } from '@qb-flex/react';

function Account() {
  const auth = useQBAuth();
  if (auth.isLoading) return pChecking your session…/p;
  if (!auth.user) return SignIn /;
  return section
    pSigned in as {auth.user.email}/p
    button onClick={() = auth.signOut().catch(console.error)}Sign out/button
  /section;
}


All components under the provider observe the same session. The hook exposes session, user, loading/error state and signIn/signUp/signOut/refresh actions.

 Sign in or register


await auth.signIn({ email, password });
await auth.signUp({ email, password, name });


Catch failures and present auth.error.message in the form. Registration may require verification before a session is available. Never assume signup always authenticates the user.

 Enable protected queries


const auth = useQBAuth();
const tasks = useQBQuery({
  queryKey: ['tasks'],
  enabled: Boolean(auth.user),
  queryFn: (_, signal) = client.from('tasks').abortSignal(signal).execute(),
});


Conditional rendering improves the interface; server policies are still the authorization boundary.

 Identity changes

Query keys include the project, branch and identity generation. The provider cancels in-flight queries and removes affected cached results when the user changes. Realtime subscriptions reauthorize under the new session.

Do not keep copies of protected query data in unrelated global state. Clear application-specific derived state on signout as well.

 Server rendering

Create clients and QueryClient instances per request. Do not reuse a module-global authenticated client on a server. Hooks do not read browser APIs during server rendering; session loading occurs after initialization. Advanced request-session hydration requires an explicit integration rather than assuming browser cookies exist on the server.

 React queries, mutations and realtime

 Query ownership

The query function receives the configured client and a cancellation signal. A query key must include every input that changes the data. The provider prefixes it with project, branch and identity scope.

 Insert a task


const auth = useQBAuth();
const addTask = useQBMutation({
  mutationFn: (_, title: string) = client.from('tasks').insert({
    title,
    owner_id: auth.user!.id,
  }, { idempotencyKey: crypto.randomUUID() }),
  invalidate: [['tasks']],
});

await addTask.mutateAsync('Write the first integration');


The hook exposes TanStack Query mutation state, including pending/error state. Generate the idempotency key once for each logical action; keep it when retrying that action manually.

 Update or delete


await client.from('tasks').update(task.id, { completed: 1 }, {
  revision: Number(task.revision),
});
await client.from('tasks').delete(task.id);


Wrap these operations in useQBMutation and invalidate the task query after success. A stale revision returns a conflict; refetch and let the user reconcile rather than overwriting blindly.

 Subscribe through a hook


const realtime = useQBRealtime({
  table: 'tasks',
  enabled: Boolean(auth.user),
  cursor: tasks.data?.meta.consistencyToken,
  onEvent: () = {},
  invalidate: [['tasks']],
});


The hook creates and disposes the subscription with the component lifecycle. It invalidates the selected queries when events arrive and exposes connection status. React Strict Mode cleanup must not leave a duplicate connection.

 Loading and failure states

Show empty lists distinctly from policy denial or network errors. Disable duplicate mutation submissions while pending. Keep user drafts when a write fails. Use query refetch for recoverable failures; denied writes need a changed identity, policy or input.

 React file handling

 Use the storage hook


import { useQBStorage } from '@qb-flex/react';

function Upload({ bucketId }: { bucketId: string }) {
  const storage = useQBStorage();
  return labelAttach a file
    input type="file" disabled={storage.isPending} onChange={async event = {
      const file = event.target.files?.[0];
      if (!file) return;
      try { await storage.upload(bucketId, file.name, file); }
      catch { / Render storage.error in the form. / }
    }} /
    {storage.error && span role="alert"{storage.error.message}/span}
  /label;
}


isPending describes transfer activity, not byte progress. Initial uploads are bounded, non-resumable transfers. Keep filename and size visible while the transfer runs.

 Download safely

Call storage.download(objectId) to receive a Blob. Create an object URL for the browser and revoke it after use. Do not expose an S3 backend URL or credentials.

 Delete and refresh

Call storage.remove(objectId) and invalidate the file-list query after success. Listing uses the same client identity. An object from another private owner is unavailable.

 Cancellation

Upload/download methods accept a request option containing an AbortSignal. Cancel operations when the user deliberately leaves the transfer flow. Cancellation does not guarantee that remote work did not commit; refresh object state before retrying.

 JavaScript authentication and CRUD

 Sign in and observe session state


await qb.auth.signUp({ email, password, name });
await qb.auth.signIn({ email, password });
const stopAuth = qb.onAuthStateChange(session = {
  accountLabel.textContent = session?.user.email ?? 'Signed out';
});
await qb.auth.refreshSession();


Always handle rejected promises and verification requirements. Sessions are shared by calls through the same client instance. SDK tokens are not automatically written to localStorage.

 Load tasks


const result = await qb.from('tasks')
  .select(['id', 'title', 'completed'])
  .eq('completed', 0)
  .limit(50);

list.replaceChildren(...result.data.map(task = {
  const item = document.createElement('li');
  item.textContent = task.title;
  return item;
}));


Use textContent for application values instead of inserting user-provided HTML.

 Insert, update and delete


const session = qb.getSession();
if (!session) throw new Error('Sign in first');
await qb.from('tasks').insert({
  owner_id: session.user.id,
  title: 'Ship the first application',
}, { idempotencyKey: crypto.randomUUID() });
await qb.from('tasks').update(taskId, { completed: 1 });
await qb.from('tasks').delete(taskId);


Ownership is validated by the server. Refetch the list after mutations so sorting, filters and counts remain consistent.

 Pagination and cancellation

Follow result.meta.cursor with the exact same query configuration. Use abortSignal(controller.signal) for a pending read and call controller.abort() when it is no longer needed.

 Error handling


try { await qb.from('tasks').insert(row); }
catch (error) { errorMessage.textContent = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Request failed'; }


Keep form input on failure. Resolve validation and policy errors before retrying. For a potentially committed mutation, reuse the original idempotency key rather than creating another action.

 JavaScript realtime and files

 Subscribe after an authorized snapshot


const snapshot = await qb.from('tasks');
const channel = qb.channel({
  table: 'tasks',
  cursor: snapshot.meta.consistencyToken,
  onEvent: () = refreshTasks(),
  onStatus: status = { connectionLabel.textContent = status; },
  onResync: () = refreshTasks(),
}).subscribe();


A subscription is an explicit resource. Dispose it when replacing the screen or leaving the application. Realtime delivery uses the current session and exposed-table policies.

 Upload a file


const file = fileInput.files?.[0];
if (file) {
  const uploaded = await qb.storage.upload(bucketId, file.name, file);
  const blob = await qb.storage.download(uploaded.data.id);
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  downloadLink.href = url;
  downloadLink.download = file.name;
  // Release after the UI no longer uses the URL:
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
}


Use an authenticated application user and a configured S3 backend. Private files are restricted to their owner.

 Lifecycle cleanup


channel.unsubscribe();
stopAuth();
qb.dispose();


Call cleanup only when the instance is no longer used. dispose closes channels and removes session listeners. A disposed client rejects later requests.

 Identity changes

Refetch and clear protected DOM state after signout. Keep every application's state scoped to its client and user; do not mix results from another project. The SDK reauthorizes its channels after identity changes, while your application remains responsible for clearing previously rendered private content.

